Slice 2 is done and every lane is green, with one exception: `cljstyle check` is still blocked by a permission prompt, so I checked its rules by hand instead. Nothing is staged or committed. The full report is at `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790751145000-storage-engineer-repl-durable-index-slice2.claude-opus-5-5.report.md`.

**Kept from glm's partial work:** most of it, including:
- the HEAD record and how it is read;
- the full walk of the snapshot at open, which refuses startup if anything is corrupt;
- the lock taken before anything else in the directory is touched;
- HEAD written only after the manifest is read back;
- the recovery handed over for slice 3;
- closing the store on exit on all three hosts;
- most of the tests.

**Fixed:**
- **Dart could open the same directory twice in one process.** The JVM and Dart OS locks never refuse their own process. The lock now also keeps a list of the directories this process holds and refuses a second owner before opening anything. Closing a store twice can no longer release a later owner's lock.
- **Node could steal a live owner's lock.** A process owned by another user was being treated as dead. It now counts as alive.
- **The JVM now syncs the directory after replacing HEAD.** Before, only Node did.
- **`:clj` came first in the reader conditionals** in `store/fs.cljc` and both test files, which is the ClojureDart trap. All now put `:cljd` first. The index tests also close their stores now.

**Added:**
- A test proving HEAD is replaced by rename, never written in place: it succeeds even when HEAD is read-only.
- A crash test: a replacement interrupted before the rename leaves HEAD whole, and a reopen recovers the previous snapshot.
- Real second-process lock tests for Node and Dart, alongside the existing JVM one.
- Doc updates.

**Evidence:**
- Each key guarantee was broken on purpose, and a test failed each time (all reverted).
- One Dart run with a deliberate failure confirmed that ClojureDart test failures really fail the run, and that the Dart child process really holds the lock.
- Lane results:
  - kondo: 0 errors, 0 warnings.
  - Focused JVM: 108 tests, 0 failures.
  - Full JVM: 2407 tests, 0 failures.
  - Node: 2312 tests, 0 failures.
  - Peer build: built.
  - Dart: all passed, and I confirmed the new tests ran by name.

**Decision for you:** until slice 3 restores the old facts on startup, a restarted or reset durable session publishes only its own facts, and HEAD moves off the previous snapshot. The old data stays in `content.jing` but nothing points to it any more. This is written in the doc and the store docstring. Don't ship slice 2 on its own as "durable".

The Claude.ai Gmail, Google Calendar and Google Drive connectors need authorizing in your claude.ai connector settings before they can be used. Nothing in this task needed them.
